## 🛠️ 3. Phase 1: Preparing the Ubuntu Bastion Host & Installing Tools

> ⚠️ **CRITICAL RULE FOR JUNIORS:** Never download or extract `aws-cli`, `eksctl`, or `kubectl` inside `/tmp`! On Ubuntu EC2 instances, `/tmp` is a tiny 455 MB RAM disk (`tmpfs`). Extracting tools inside `/tmp` fills it to 100% and corrupts the binaries! Always run installations from `/root` or `/home/ubuntu`.

### Step 1: Switch to Root and Move to `/root`
```bash
sudo su -
cd /root
pwd
# Expected Output: /root
```

### Step 2: Check Available Disk Space
Make sure `/` (`/dev/root`) has at least 2 GB of free space (`Avail` column):
```bash
df -h /root /tmp
```

### Step 3: Verify `aws-cli` is Installed
```bash
aws --version
# Expected Output: aws-cli/2.x.x Python/3.x.x Linux/...
```

### Step 4: Install `eksctl` Cleanly in `/root`
```bash
cd /root
rm -f /usr/local/bin/eksctl
ARCH=amd64
PLATFORM=$(uname -s)_$ARCH
curl -sLO "[https://github.com/eksctl-io/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz](https://github.com/eksctl-io/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz)"
tar -xzf eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz -C /usr/local/bin
rm -f eksctl_$PLATFORM.tar.gz

# Verify installation
eksctl version
# Expected Output: 0.231.0 (or latest version)
```

### Step 5: Install `kubectl` Cleanly in `/root`
```bash
cd /root
curl -LO "[https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl](https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl) -L -s [https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl](https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl)"
install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl
rm -f kubectl

# Verify installation
kubectl version --client
# Expected Output: Client Version: v1.3x.x
```

