## 🛠️ 7. Real-World Troubleshooting Scenarios (What Went Wrong & How We Fixed It)

During our setup, we encountered **4 real-world errors**. If you see any of these errors during your practice, follow the exact fix below:

---

### Problem 1: `Segmentation fault` When Running `eksctl` (`/tmp` RAM Disk Full)

**❌ Error Message Seen:**
```text
root@ip-172-31-12-85:/tmp/eks-tools# df -h /tmp /usr/local/bin /root
Filesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
tmpfs           455M  455M     0 100% /tmp
/dev/root       6.7G  3.0G  3.7G  45% /

root@ip-172-31-12-85:/tmp/eks-tools# eksctl version
Segmentation fault         eksctl version
```

**🔍 Why It Happened:**
We downloaded and extracted `aws-cli` and `eksctl` inside `/tmp/eks-tools`. Because `/tmp` is mounted on a small **455 MB RAM disk (`tmpfs`)**, it hit **100% full (`0 Avail`)** while extracting `eksctl`. Only half of the `eksctl` binary was copied to `/usr/local/bin/eksctl`, causing Linux to crash with a `Segmentation fault` whenever we ran it.

**✅ How We Solved It:**
1. Deleted all temporary files inside `/tmp` and `~/.cache`:
   ```bash
   rm -rf ~/.cache/* /tmp/*
   ```
2. Moved out of `/tmp` into `/root` (which has 3.7 GB of real disk space on `/dev/root`):
   ```bash
   cd /root
   rm -f /usr/local/bin/eksctl
   ```
3. Re-downloaded and extracted `eksctl` and `kubectl` directly from `/root` (see [Phase 1](#-3-phase-1-preparing-the-ubuntu-bastion-host--installing-tools)).

---

### Problem 2: `AlreadyExistsException: Stack [eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster] already exists`

**❌ Error Message Seen:**
```text
2026-10-21 21:11:35 [✖] creating CloudFormation stack "eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster": operation error CloudFormation: CreateStack, StatusCode: 400, AlreadyExistsException: Stack [eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster] already exists
Error: failed to create cluster "my-eks-cluster"
```

**🔍 Why It Happened:**
While our first `eksctl create cluster` command was already running (or after the Control Plane stack had already been created), `eksctl create cluster --name my-eks-cluster` was accidentally executed a second time[cite: 4]. CloudFormation blocked it because a stack named `eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster` already existed in `ap-south-1`[cite: 4].

**✅ How We Solved It:**
Instead of deleting the whole cluster and wasting 15 minutes, we checked the status of our existing CloudFormation stacks:
```bash
aws cloudformation list-stacks \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --stack-status-filter CREATE_IN_PROGRESS CREATE_COMPLETE ROLLBACK_IN_PROGRESS ROLLBACK_COMPLETE CREATE_FAILED \
  --query "StackSummaries[?starts_with(StackName, 'eksctl-my-eks-cluster')].[StackName,StackStatus]" \
  --output table
```
We saw that **`eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster` was already `CREATE_COMPLETE`**, meaning our EKS Control Plane was healthy and ready! We only needed to fix the worker nodegroup.

---

### Problem 3: Nodegroup Stuck in `ROLLBACK_COMPLETE` (AWS Free Tier Instance Restriction)

**❌ Error Message Seen:**
When checking our stacks, the Control Plane succeeded, but the worker nodegroup failed:
```text
+---------------------------------------------------+---------------------+
|  eksctl-my-eks-cluster-nodegroup-standard-workers |  ROLLBACK_COMPLETE  |
|  eksctl-my-eks-cluster-cluster                    |  CREATE_COMPLETE    |
+---------------------------------------------------+---------------------+
```
To find out **why** it rolled back, we ran:
```bash
aws cloudformation describe-stack-events \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --stack-name eksctl-my-eks-cluster-nodegroup-standard-workers \
  --query "StackEvents[?ResourceStatus=='CREATE_FAILED'].[LogicalResourceId,ResourceStatusReason]" \
  --output table
```
Which revealed the exact AWS error:
> `AsgInstanceLaunchFailures: Could not launch On-Demand Instances. InvalidParameterCombination - The specified instance type is not eligible for Free Tier. For a list of Free Tier instance types, run 'describe-instance-types' with the filter 'free-tier-eligible=true'.`

**🔍 Why It Happened:**
We initially requested `--node-type t3.medium`[cite: 2]. However, our AWS account is restricted to **Free Tier-eligible instance types only**, so AWS refused to launch `t3.medium` EC2 instances and rolled back the nodegroup stack after 23 minutes[cite: 2, 3].

**✅ How We Solved It:**
1. We queried AWS for Free Tier-eligible instances in `ap-south-1` (see [Phase 2, Step 2](#step-2-check-allowed-free-tier-instance-types-in-your-region)) and discovered **`c7i-flex.large`** (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM) is Free Tier-eligible!
2. We deleted the failed nodegroup stack (see Problem 4 below) and used **`eksctl create nodegroup`** (instead of `create cluster`) to attach `c7i-flex.large` nodes directly to our already-running Control Plane in just **3 minutes**[cite: 4, 5]:
   ```bash
   eksctl create nodegroup \
     --cluster my-eks-cluster \
     --region ap-south-1 \
     --name standard-workers \
     --node-type c7i-flex.large \
     --nodes 2 \
     --nodes-min 1 \
     --nodes-max 3 \
     --managed
   ```

