## 🧹 8. Phase 5: Scaling Nodes & Daily Teardown (Save AWS Costs!)

### Option A: Scale Down to 1 Worker Node (While Practicing)
If you want to keep your cluster running for a few hours while using fewer AWS Free Tier EC2 hours, scale your nodegroup from 2 nodes down to 1 node:
```bash
eksctl scale nodegroup \
  --cluster my-eks-cluster \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --name standard-workers \
  --nodes 1 \
  --nodes-min 1 \
  --nodes-max 3
```
*Verify after 1 minute:*
```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

### Option B: Complete Cluster Deletion (End of Every Practice Session)
> ⚠️ **MANDATORY FOR JUNIORS:** The EKS Control Plane ($0.10/hour) and AWS NAT Gateway are **NOT free**. Always delete the entire EKS cluster at the end of your daily practice session!

```bash
# 1. Delete any Kubernetes LoadBalancer services first (so AWS deletes the ELB)
kubectl delete svc nginx-web --ignore-not-found

# 2. Delete the entire EKS cluster (Nodegroup + Control Plane + VPC + NAT Gateway)
eksctl delete cluster --name my-eks-cluster --region ap-south-1
```

### Verify Everything Is Deleted Before Logging Off
Run this command after `eksctl delete cluster` finishes to be 100% sure no stacks remain:
```bash
aws cloudformation list-stacks \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --stack-status-filter CREATE_COMPLETE UPDATE_COMPLETE ROLLBACK_COMPLETE DELETE_FAILED \
  --query "StackSummaries[?starts_with(StackName, 'eksctl-')].[StackName,StackStatus]" \
  --output table
```
*(When the table returns empty, all EKS resources are completely removed!)*
